            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) and each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exist three distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nk & \\text{family of three lines} & \\text{sunny lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & \\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=1\\} & \\text{none}\\\\\n1 & \\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\} & \\{y=x\\}\\\\\n2 & \\{x=2,\\;y=x,\\;y=x+1\\} & \\{y=x,\\;y=x+1\\}\\\\\n3 & \\{y=x,\\;y=\\tfrac12x,\\;y=2x-1\\} & \\text{all three}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nEach family consists of three distinct lines, the indicated number of them\nhas slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), and a direct check shows that\nevery point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one line of the family. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All‑sunny construction).\n\nFor any \\(n\\ge 3\\) the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = x + (i-1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll \\(L_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); hence they are sunny.\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\).\nChoose \\(i=b\\) (note that \\(1\\le b\\le n\\)). Then\n\n\\[\ny = x + (i-1) = x + (b-1)\n\\]\n\nand substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y = a + b -1 = b\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n\\(L_{b}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive proof of the theorem\n\n**Theorem.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n*Base.*  \nThe statement holds for \\(n=3\\) by Lemma 1.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the theorem true for some \\(n-1\\ge 3\\); i.e. for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there exist \\(n-1\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(k\\le n-1\\).*  \nApply the inductive hypothesis to the pair \\((n-1,k)\\).  \nWe obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, such that every point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=n .\n\\]\n\n\\(V\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and it is distinct from all\nlines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) (all those lines intersect the strip \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\)).\nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=\\mathcal F\\cup\\{V\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\nThe points of \\(T_{n}\\setminus T_{n-1}\\) are precisely those with\n\\(a=n\\) and \\(b=1\\) (the single point \\((n,1)\\)), because the inequality\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le 1\\) when \\(a=n\\).  \nThe added line \\(V\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\); consequently every point of\n\\(T_{n}\\) belongs to some line of \\(\\mathcal F'\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\le n-1\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) sunny lines described in Lemma 2:\n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = x + (i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThey are all sunny, distinct, and by Lemma 2 they cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\nHence a family with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nBoth cases together show that for the given \\(n\\) a family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nSince a family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines,\nno larger value of \\(k\\) is possible. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the\nnon‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
