### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have repaired the proof.  \nThe correct answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition **iff** the number of sunny lines is either \\(0\\) or \\(n\\).  \nBoth possibilities can be realised, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Step 1 – Geometry of the point set.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_{n}\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),\\,(n,1),\\,(1,n)\\).\n\n*Step 2 – Two explicit constructions.*\n\n* **All‑non‑sunny case (\\(k=0\\)).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently it lies on the line \\(x=a\\). Thus the \\(n\\) vertical lines cover \\(T_{n}\\) and contain no sunny line.\n\n* **All‑sunny case (\\(k=n\\)).**  \nConsider the \\(n\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = x + (i-1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\).  \nChoosing \\(i=b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = x + (b-1),\n\\]\n\nand substituting \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y = a+b-1 = b\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Step 3 – Impossibility of intermediate values of \\(k\\).*  \n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) contains exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(0<k<n\\).  \nLet the sunny lines be \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) and the non‑sunny lines be\n\\(N_{1},\\dots ,N_{n-k}\\).\n\nEvery non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions\n(\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nConsequently each non‑sunny line can intersect the triangle \\(T_{n}\\) in at most\n\\(n\\) lattice points (a vertical line meets at most \\(n\\) points, a horizontal\nline at most \\(n\\) points, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) at most \\(n\\) points).\n\nHence the total number of lattice points that can be covered by the\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\).\n\nOn the other hand, the triangle \\(T_{n}\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nlattice points (the triangular numbers).  \nSince \\(k\\ge 1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n \\;<\\; n^{2} \\;\\le\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_{n}|\n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nso the non‑sunny lines alone cannot cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nTherefore at least one of the remaining points must be covered by a sunny\nline.  \n\nEach sunny line, having a slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\), can pass through\nat most **one** lattice point of \\(T_{n}\\) that is not already covered by a\nnon‑sunny line (otherwise two distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\) would lie on a line\nwith slope \\(0,-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\), which is impossible).  \nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) additional points.\n\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered by the whole\nfamily is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n + k \\;<\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_{n}|\n\\qquad\\text{whenever }0<k<n,\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Hence no configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) exists.\n\n*Step 4 – Conclusion.*  \nThe only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), and both are\nrealised by the constructions of Step 2. ∎\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to\nthe \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and from \\(-1\\)\n(parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (All‑non‑sunny construction).\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point of \\(T_{n}\\)\nlies on exactly one of them.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence the point satisfies the\nequation \\(x=a\\) and therefore belongs to the line \\(x=a\\).  All the lines are\nparallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All‑sunny construction).\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = x + (i-1).\n\\]\n\nThen the \\(L_{i}\\) are distinct sunny lines and together they contain every\npoint of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll \\(L_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); thus each is sunny.\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\nChoosing \\(i=b\\) gives the line \\(y = x + (b-1)\\); substituting \\(x=a\\) yields\n\\(y = a+b-1 = b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound for intermediate \\(k\\)).\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(0<k<n\\), then\n\n\\[\n|T_{n}| \\le (n-k)n + k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\nA line of any of those three directions can intersect the integer lattice\ninside the triangle \\(T_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the maximal number of\nlattice points on a vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) segment inside the\ntriangle is \\(n\\)).  Hence the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most\n\\((n-k)n\\) points.\n\nA sunny line has slope different from the three forbidden values;