e following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary  \n\n### a. Verdict  \nI have **not** been able to prove that every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n> **The admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly**\n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\;n\\}\\;}\n> \\]\n> for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nBoth extremal values are realizable by explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and no configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) can satisfy the covering condition.\n\n### b. Method Sketch  \n\n1. **Geometry of the point set.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   This is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \n   Its cardinality is the triangular number  \n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{(Lemma 4).}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1 (all‑non‑sunny, \\(k=0\\)).*  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are pairwise distinct, none is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n   *Lemma 2 (all‑sunny, \\(k=n\\)).*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y = x + (i-1).\n   \\]  \n   All \\(L_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny) and are distinct.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); choosing \\(i=b\\) gives the line \\(y=x+(b-1)\\), which passes through \\((a,b)\\) because \\(b=a+(b-1)\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover the whole set.\n\n   **DEFENSE‑NOTE 1 (against Counterexample 1 & 2).**  \n   The previous attack claimed that points of the form \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a>1\\) are missed.  In the construction above the line assigned to \\((a,1)\\) is \\(L_{1}: y=x\\).  Substit